+//! P17 half A, decision 02: "sunk -> the terrain wins the depth test, the plate is simply hidden".
+//! That sentence is only true while the terrain the depth test uses is the LOD 0 heightfield. From LOD 1
+//! outwards the terrain mesh samples the heightmap every 2^level units, so between two sampled nodes it
+//! draws the CHORD over whatever is in between: a decimated surface that dips locally below the real
+//! ground by up to m_geomError, which is metre class on any interesting terrain (@see research 11 1.2).
+//! Wherever that dip goes under a sunken plate, the plate's own darker material wins the depth test and
+//! pokes through the ground in a scalloped band that grows with distance. It is not a shadow, not the
+//! bake (Raise mode never touches the terrain under a sunken plate) and not the base pass; the plate is
+//! simply visible where it was designed to be invisible.
+//! The engine already has the exact semantic for "do not draw this in camera passes, but keep everything
+//! else": ERF_HIDDEN_FROM_CAMERA (IRenderNode.h:225), tested in CBrush::Render (Brush.cpp:158, :820)
+//! only when the pass is neither a shadow pass nor a recursive one - so shadows, the static shadow
+//! cache, HeightMap AO, SVOGI voxelisation, physics, the navmesh and the editor's CPU hit test all keep
